{% comment %}
  A navigation label, optionally carrying a line of subtext.

  Shopify menu items cannot hold metafields -- a linklist link is not a
  metafield-owning resource -- so the subtext is written into the link TITLE in
  the admin, after a pipe:

      The SUF-CPT | start here — Level 1

  and rendered as

      The SUF-CPT<span class="suf-navsub">start here — Level 1</span>

  WHY THE TITLE AND NOT A THEME SETTING. The alternative is header section
  blocks pairing a handle to a subtitle, which gives the merchant proper fields
  but two lists to keep in step: rename a menu item and its subtitle silently
  orphans. Here the label and its subtext are one string, edited in the same
  place, and they cannot drift apart.

  The cost is a pipe visible in the admin menu editor. That is the whole cost.

  NO PIPE, NO SPAN. A title without one renders exactly as before, so every
  existing menu item is unaffected and the subtext is opt-in per link.

  `contains` is tested first because `split` always returns at least one part
  -- parts.size can never distinguish "has a subtitle" from "does not".

  Everything is escaped. The merchant is typing into a menu field, and the pipe
  is the only markup this snippet lets through.

  Usage:
    {% render 'suf-navlabel', title: child_link.title %}
{% endcomment %}

{%- assign raw = title | default: '' | strip -%}

{%- if raw contains '|' -%}
  {%- assign parts = raw | split: '|' -%}
  {%- assign label = parts[0] | strip -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    Rejoined rather than taking parts[1]: a subtitle is allowed to contain a
    pipe of its own, and only the FIRST one separates label from subtext.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign sub = parts | slice: 1, 99 | join: '|' | strip -%}
  {{- label | escape -}}
  {%- if sub != blank -%}
    <span class="suf-navsub">{{ sub | escape }}</span>
  {%- endif -%}
{%- else -%}
  {{- raw | escape -}}
{%- endif -%}
